Delegating AD DS connector permissions to svc-entraconnect — batch-logon mechanism PROVEN,
delegation itself INCOMPLETE (stalled, not failed).

WHY A SCHEDULED TASK AT ALL. The three permission cmdlets (Set-ADSyncBasicReadPermissions,
Set-ADSyncMsDsConsistencyGuidPermissions, Set-ADSyncPasswordHashSyncPermissions) have no
-Credential parameter — their "...WithCredential" parameter sets refer to TargetForestCredential,
for cross-forest scenarios only. Captured via:

  (Get-Command <cmd>).ParameterSets / .Parameters.Keys
  -> ADConnectorAccountName, ADConnectorAccountDomain, ADConnectorAccountDN, ADobjectDN,
     IncludeAdminSDHolders, TargetForest, TargetForestCredential, ...
  Only Set-ADSyncRestrictedPermissions has a real -Credential (and it is required there).

So they run in whatever security context invokes them, and need rights to modify domain-root ACLs.
But VM102, now that it is genuinely domain-joined, receives Secure Admin WS — verified:

  gpresult /r /scope:computer on ENTRACONNECT01 -> Applied GPOs: Default Domain Policy,
    District Lockdown, Secure Admin WS
  secedit /export /areas USER_RIGHTS on VM102 ->
    SeBatchLogonRight           = *S-1-5-32-544,*S-1-5-32-551,*S-1-5-32-559
    SeInteractiveLogonRight     = *S-1-5-32-544,*S-1-5-32-545,*S-1-5-32-551
    SeDenyInteractiveLogonRight = *S-1-5-21-2288391267-384259729-2991373820-512  (Domain Admins)
    (no SeDenyBatchLogonRight at all)

  DISTRICT\Administrator cannot log on interactively to VM102. Batch logon is NOT denied, and
  Domain Admins holds it via BUILTIN\Administrators (544). A scheduled task runs under batch
  logon — hence the approach.

  NOTE ON WHAT THIS IS AND IS NOT: batch logon still places the credential in LSASS on VM102.
  This is not a credential-exposure-free bypass. Its defensible property is scope — one process,
  one run, then unregistered — versus an open-ended GPO exception. Argue it on bounded scope and
  reversibility, not on "it's secure."

